{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785092247,"nonce":"vpoI1tuSMsNfXtWyFC3UToJy1Qg=","title":"ADR-0061: tighten workspace-service blob reads from authenticated to authorized","message":"s1 ships the workspace token with an honest but incomplete authorization\nstory, and the gap must be a ticket rather than a doc comment that implies\nmore than the code does.\n\nDepends on: s1.\n\n---\n\n\nThe workspace token is `wsv1.{exp_unix}.{scope}.{roots_b64url}.{sha256=\u003chex\u003e}`\nover the message\n`format!(\"wsv1|{run}|{step}|{attempt}|{exp_unix}|{scope}|{roots}\")`, where\n`scope` is `read` or `browse` and `roots` is the sorted, `,`-joined input\nroots. Header `x-scarab-workspace-token`. Enforcement in the service:\n\n- **Trees** — `GET /v1/cas/trees/{hash}` and `/flat`: `{hash}` MUST appear in\n  the token's `roots`, unless `scope=browse`. Enforced, cheap, exact. Because\n  `/flat` returns the whole subtree in one call, a Pod never needs to walk\n  sub-trees, so this allowlist is sufficient in practice.\n- **Blobs** — `GET /v1/cas/blobs/{hash}`: **any valid token.** The\n  justification, which s1 writes into the code: a blob name is 256\n  unguessable bits and is only learnable from a tree the token was already\n  allowed to read.\n- **Writes** — `PUT` of blobs/trees: any valid `read`-or-`write` token. Safe\n  by construction: a content-addressed write with a verified hash cannot\n  overwrite or corrupt anything. Worst case is disk consumption, which is the\n  warm tier's bounded resource.\n\n\nBlob reads are fence-**authenticated**, not fence-**authorized**. The\ndifference is real, not pedantic:\n\n- The unguessability argument is about *discovery*, and it holds only while\n  hashes stay secret. A hash that leaks (a log line, a UI URL, an error\n  message, a shared build cache, an artifact manifest) becomes a permanent\n  cross-tenant read capability, because the token has no notion of *which*\n  content it may read — only that it is a valid token for *some* Step.\n- Any valid token from any tenant's Step Pod can read any blob in the cluster\n  whose hash it learns. That is a weaker boundary than every other fence in\n  Scarab (ADR-0042's results fence is per `{run, step, attempt}`).\n- It is an availability lever too: a token that never needed content can pull\n  arbitrary bytes through the service.\n\n\nMake a blob read *reachability-checked*, not merely authenticated: the\nservice must be able to answer \"is `{blob}` reachable from a tree in this\ntoken's `roots`?\" cheaply.\n\nSketch, to be designed not assumed:\n\n- When the service answers `/flat` for a root, it already computed the\n  complete blob set for that root. Cache that set keyed by root (it is\n  immutable, so the cache never invalidates) and admit a blob read only if it\n  is in the union of the token's roots' sets. Turns the check into a set\n  membership test, no walk per request.\n- Memory cost is bounded by the number of *live tokens'* distinct roots, not\n  by the store; evict with the same lease as the token `exp`.\n- `scope=browse` tokens (minted by the server for itself, never handed to\n  Pods) keep unrestricted read — the control plane already holds\n  object-store credentials, so restricting it buys nothing.\n\nAlso fix while here: the s1 service emits a `tracing::warn!` with run/step on\nevery 401 (the results endpoint emits nothing on failure, and that is a gap,\nnot a pattern). Extend the same treatment to a *rejected* blob read once the\ncheck exists, so the tightening is observable rather than silent.\n\n\n- A token whose `roots` do not reach `{hash}` gets 403 (not 404 — do not\n  turn authorization into an oracle unless a deliberate decision says so),\n  with a warn line naming run/step.\n- The check costs no extra round trip on the hot path.\n- The doc comment in the service stops saying \"fence-authenticated, not\n  fence-authorized\" and starts describing what is actually enforced.","files":null}]}